From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 02:07:42 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 21/30] Add a VSX path for the Skia convolution filter on
 PowerPC

The image downscaling convolution in gfx/2d had SSE2, AVX2 and NEON kernels
and fell back to the scalar loops on PowerPC. ConvolutionFilterVSX.cpp adds
the VSX kernels, selected in SkConvolver.cpp through supports_vsx().

The horizontal kernel permutes each pair of pixels into channel-ordered
16-bit lanes ([r0 r1 g0 g1 b0 b1 a0 a1]) so that one vec_msum against the
coefficient pair [c0 c1 c0 c1 ...] accumulates two pixels per channel; four
coefficients cost two permutes and two multiply-sums. The vertical kernel
interleaves two source rows with vec_mergeh/vec_mergel and accumulates both
rows per vec_msum, with an odd trailing row weighted [c 0]. Rounding,
shifting and the signed-then-unsigned saturating packs match the SSE2
kernel bit for bit, and the alpha fix-up (alpha = max(alpha, r, g, b), or
0xFF for opaque formats) uses three word rotates and a byte select.

The partial last block of the vertical kernel loads 16 bytes like the SSE2
kernel does, which the 32-byte row padding from PadBytesForSIMD allows. The
byte-order dependent steps (zero extension, alpha mask) are selected with
std::endian so the kernels work on big-endian targets too. The horizontal
reference test in TestConvolutionFilter now also runs against the VSX
kernel.
